# dao.stream.serve invariant revision — round 2 cross-read (GLM-5.3)

I opened every line codex cites (revision:42-184; original design:91-234; `dao.stream.md:263-272, 389, 674`; `dao.stream.discovery.md:70, 159`). One of its findings moves a settled position of mine (Q2), one exposes a defect I missed in both drafts (the ws-identity/peer-id conflation), and the rest I hold or adopt as marked.

## 1. Q2 and Q3

**Q2 — CHANGE: defer held reads from v1 (codex's position; the earlier consensus restored), with two spec edits.** I revised my round-1 endorsement of Fable's necessity claim, and the directed question is where it breaks: reachability through the meeting peer makes polling a standing cost bounded in **rate** but not in **duration** — the `:max-attempts` budget bounds retries of an *unanswered* request (`findings.md:194`), while a blocked inbox demand is *answered* (blocked) and then re-issued per the composition's cadence policy (`findings.md:137`), which can only be configured, never exhausted, without ending reachability. So codex is right against the word "busy loop" and wrong that a *budget* bounds the cost; the truth is a tunable standing poll. What decides the disposition is that **reachability does not condition on holds**: no acceptance criterion requires them (revised §13 items 5-6 need only correctness, not latency; the UCF facade test tolerates poll latency), the coupling between inbound latency and traffic is composition data, and `:serve/hold` was designed additive with both tolerance directions mandated (`findings.md:131`). My round-1 "include" was premised on necessity; that premise fails on the evidence above, so I hold the deferral — not to converge. Two edits ride with it (items 13 and 5 below): the honest-costs sentence stating the coupling, and `:serve/hold` retained as the designed relief valve with its enabling condition named (the first deployment class where the latency/traffic coupling bites). Fable's revised Q2 should not stand; the consensus Q2 stands, now with the reachability profile stated honestly.

**Q3 — AGREE in substance; one sentence resolves it, as the orchestrator suspects.** Codex and my round-1 already agree on everything operative: the peer's *own composition* mints the id (no meeting peer assigns — I wrote "there is no assigner… minted by the peer itself" in round 1), verification is additive, duplicate `:meet/here` handling is convention policy, and a hash without proof of control binds nothing. The residual is only what "intended format" may be read to claim. One sentence, which I propose as the spec text: "A peer id is minted by the peer's own composition; v1 ids are opaque 128-bit random values, and a peer may adopt the self-certifying form — the hash of its public key — so that the later additive verification rule can bind id to key without re-minting, but until that rule exists an id of either form is an unauthenticated name: nothing in this protocol checks key control, and a key-hash id must not be read as a binding (`dao.stream.md:1087-1092`)." The reason to keep the permissive key-hash form at all (against codex's flat "opaque random"): ids travel inside serve descriptors and UCF cells; changing id format after descriptors are in circulation invalidates in-flight references in a repo where nothing else needs migration. Choosing the format once is cheap insurance, and the sentence strips the false inference codex correctly fears.

## 2. Codex's extra defects, adjudicated

- **Restricted handles — PARTLY true; needs a sentence, not a mechanism.** The need (M reads requests privately while visitors only write; M writes announcements while visitors only read) is real, but the design already has the mechanism: the serve table maps `identity → {:handle h :surface S}` — the surface is per-entry data (`findings.md:160`), and the derivation is "restricted to S" (`findings.md:91`). M declares `#{:writer}` for the request stream's entry and `#{:reader}` for the announcement stream's entry while retaining its own full local handles. The edit (item 9) states this explicitly so no implementer assumes the served surface must equal the handle's surface. Codex's citation of `dao.stream.md:272` (attachment correlation delivered to the composition) is accurate and belongs to item 3.
- **Outer-gap versus final-gap — TRUE; needs the edit.** This sharpens what Fable's "the inner retry policy absorbs" and my round-1 "absorbed via idempotence" glossed: an outer inbox `gap` is loss of *channel frames*, never eviction in the final served stream. The inner driver must advance the outer cursor to the recovery cursor, re-issue outstanding inner requests under their existing ids (reads are idempotent, `findings.md:109`), recover inner appends under the payload's dedup (OD-2), and must never relay the outer gap as a `next` outcome — the proxy "never produces a `gap` the source did not" (`findings.md:154`; `dao.stream.md:674`). Codex's replacement wording is right; adopt as item 6.
- **UDP payload budget — PARTLY true; needs the accounting sentence.** The arithmetic is real (double CBOR framing shrinks the 1200-byte datagram budget, `findings.md:193`), but the behavior is already defined — a value that cannot fit one frame is refused as `:oversize`, never silently skipped (`findings.md:121`), and fragmentation is a documented deferral. The edit (item 7) is one sentence: a relayed channel's declared frame budget is the underlying channel's budget minus outer framing overhead, so inner servers bound batches to it.
- **Request fairness — TRUE; needs the edit.** Inbox caps alone do not bound interpreter work, announcement retention, or fanout (`dao.stream.md:674` for the gap honesty side); and a silently dropped `:meet/here` leaves the requester polling announcements forever. M's meeting interpreter needs a bounded request budget, bounded work per driver step, and an *observable* refusal (an announcement fact such as `{:meet/refused <peer-id> :meet/why …}`, or a requester-side timeout feeding my re-read discipline). Merges with my retention-sizing and Q4 additions into item 5.
- **Codex's §6.2 identity wording — TRUE, and it catches a defect I missed in both drafts.** Making the ws channel's `:dao.stream/identity` the *peer id* (revision §6.2, and already the original at `findings.md:183, 212-215`) is not vocabulary: two distinct channels to the same peer (ws and udp) would then share one logical identity, and `cursor-mismatch` compares exactly that identity (`dao.stream.md:526-528`) — a cursor minted on one channel's stream would pass identity comparison on the other and be fed to a stream that never minted it, defeating OD-3's cursor-binding rule. The descriptor's identity must name *that channel's logical stream*, with the peer id under `:serve/peer` (`dao.stream.md:263-267`). Adopt codex's wording, extended to §7.1's candidates (item 8).

## 3. Merged, de-duplicated spec-edit list

| # | Section | Edit | Why |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | §6.3/§7.4 | Punch through the **same UDP socket** whose reflexive address M observed; the udp seam gains send-to-explicit-destination on an existing socket; the direct channel is the attachment that socket acquires; a **nonce-bearing pong**, not the first inbound datagram, confirms the path | A fresh `attach!` binds a fresh port ⇒ fresh NAT mapping under every NAT type; nonce defeats off-path/spoofed confirmation (`findings.md:192`; both models found this) |
| 2 | §13 crit. 6 | NAT simulator must be **restricted-cone** | A full-cone simulator passes a degraded punch |
| 3 | §7.3 | Attribute a reflexive address only via an explicit association of the request's `:serve/session` with the channel attachment event (both media carry it); a bare peer-id claim supplies no address | The serve layer never inserts attachment data into values (`findings.md:273`; `dao.stream.md:272`) |
| 4 | §7.3 | Requests carry a correlation id; on `append-unknown` for `:meet/here`, re-read announcements from a cursor minted before the append before re-requesting | Append is non-idempotent; a new session is a new dedup scope (`findings.md:113-115`; `dao.stream.md:496-499`) |
| 5 | §7.3/§12 | Announcement retention exceeds intended reader lag (mirror `dao.lease.md:259`); M's interpreter declares bounded request budget, work per step, inbox count/bytes/idle caps, and **observable** refusal (`:meet/refused` fact or requester timeout) | Gap honesty (`dao.stream.md:674`); bounded state; silent refusal makes requesters poll forever (merges codex fairness + my retention + Q4) |
| 6 | §6.4 | An outer inbox `gap` is channel loss: advance the outer cursor to the recovery cursor, re-issue outstanding inner requests under existing ids, recover appends under payload dedup; never report it as the final stream's `gap` | `findings.md:154, 109`; OD-2 (codex's wording adopted) |
| 7 | §6.4 | A relayed channel's declared frame budget = underlying budget minus outer framing overhead; inner servers batch to it | Double framing vs the 1200-byte budget (`findings.md:121, 193`) |
| 8 | §6.2/§7.1 | A channel descriptor's `:dao.stream/identity` names that channel's logical stream; the peer id rides under `:serve/peer`; distinct channels have distinct identities | Shared identity across channels defeats `cursor-mismatch` binding (`dao.stream.md:263-267, 526-528`) — pre-existing in both drafts |
| 9 | §5/§3.3 | A serve-table entry may declare a surface narrower than the handle's; the composer retains complementary local handles | Meeting streams' asymmetric access; already implied by `{:handle h :surface S}` (`findings.md:160, 91`), now stated |
| 10 | §7.3/§12 | `:meet/seen` reflexives on a shared stream are readable by all — either state the exposure in honest costs or deliver the caller's reflexive via the called peer's own inbox; peer ids are unauthenticated names until the additive verification rule exists | Regression vs the original addressed `:serve/introduce` (`findings.md:232`); `dao.stream.md:1087-1092` |
| 11 | §9/§13 crit. 8 | UCF migration acceptance pins M's inbox policy (count, retention, idle lifetime) for the test duration | Adequate lifetime spans exporter entry **and** M's inbox (Q6 note) |
| 12 | §13 crit. 10 | Codex's replacement: same serve/proxy implementation everywhere, meeting interpreter optional with explicit resource policy; tests cover two dial-only peers, loss/reordering at both framing layers, bounded meeting work, announcement gaps, fallback | Merges Fable's symmetry proof with the operable test list |
| 13 | §7.3 honest costs | "Inbound delivery through a meeting peer is demand-polled; the coupling of inbound latency to traffic is composition policy. `:serve/hold` (§3.7) is the designed additive relief, enabled where a deployment class needs the coupling broken" | Q2's disposition stated in the spec rather than discovered in operation |

No position here was moved to converge: Q2 moved because my own necessity premise failed on the retry-policy evidence; everything else is held or adopted on the citations shown.

